body:
Agentic authoring (deck form): both `kalide init` and `kalide init <path>` give the deck a coding-agent author guide, delivered as a kalide-owned full guide file `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` at the project root together with a project-root pointer `AGENTS.md` created carrying the embedded pointer content (agent-guide-reference). `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is a single Markdown file giving coding agents a complete, accurate reference to the current kalide deck and template format so they can author decks without guessing; it is written when absent, and an existing `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is kalide-owned and brought forward by `kalide upgrade` — `kalide init` never clobbers it (upgrade-refresh-owned-scaffold). `AGENTS.md` is written per the reference rule: created carrying the embedded pointer content (agent-guide-reference) when absent, left byte-for-byte untouched when it already contains the string, and otherwise appended to with the reference line while the author content is byte-preserved (agent-guide-reference). Both deck forms write the SAME guide pair: the format is identical whether the deck's library is the deck-root `templates/` (no-arg form), a configured external `templates:` path (`kalide init <path>`), or both layers together (layered-template-library); the forms differ only in which library layers the deck points at and that the external form writes an empty `slides/` with no starter slide, and the guide states all of it. `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is written verbatim from a static, authored file embedded in internal/scaffold alongside the hello seed. It is text-only, offline, and NOT a template asset: it sits at the deck root, is not part of the deck or library layout, and is never read by the load/validate/render/serve/list code paths, so it does not affect `kalide start` (global.constraint.no-embedded-template-assets, requirements.constraint.brand-not-embedded).

The guide MUST be a complete, accurate reference for the format as implemented, covering (each item traceable to the named requirement):
- the deck layout: `kalide.yaml`, `slides/`, `assets/`, and the deck's library layers — the external library named by the `templates:` key and the deck-root `templates/`, either or both of which the deck loads (external-template-library, layered-template-library);
- the layered library: the deck's library is one merged set drawn from up to two layers — the external library named by `templates:` and the deck-root `templates/` — both loaded when both are present, with equal standing and no precedence and no overriding: a name carried in both layers (a slide or section in the shared template namespace, a theme, or a shared `media/` path) is a validation error naming the name and both paths (layered-template-library, library-layer-uniqueness); a local template's `section` helper call resolves against the merged section namespace so a local template invokes an external section template like a local one, while every other reference (the `media` helper, `media:`, `theme:<name>/`) resolves within the referencing template's or theme's own layer, and the external layer is self-contained — every reference inside an external template or theme resolves within the external library alone, so the external library validates standalone (library-layer-references);
- `kalide.yaml` keys: `title` (required), `author`, `date` (YYYY-MM-DD), `theme` (default `default`), `navigation` (default|linear|grid), `properties`, `templates` (deck-config, navigation-mode, deck-properties);
- slide files: YAML frontmatter at the very top of the file (line 1) with `template:` naming a slide template, body after the closing `---`; slide naming and ordering; vertical slides; unique slide labels; sections and their frontmatter; speaker notes (slide-template-required, slide-file-ordering, vertical-slides, unique-slide-labels, slide-sections, section-frontmatter, speaker-notes);
- the `templates/` library layout: `library.yaml`, `slides/<name>/`, `sections/<name>/`, `themes/<name>/`, `media/`, and the unknown-entry rule (templates-dir-layout);
- `template.yaml` schema: `description`, `fields` (name, type, required, default, limits, variants, formats/default format, description), `sections` (name, accepted templates, min/max) and the `body` rule; `usage` is not a template key; `example.md` as the example (template-manifest, field-types, body-rules, template-composition, template-variants, number-date-formats);
- `layout.html.tmpl`: standard Go html/template only; the helper set is `media "<path>"` (path relative to `media/` and staying within it; requirements.constraint.media-paths-library-relative), `section "<name>" [fields] [body] [child…]`, `dict k1 v1 …`, `list a b …`, `child "<section>" [fields] [body] [child…]` — its first argument naming a declared section of the `section` call's target, its template chosen by an optional `template` key among its fields (child-constructor) — and `children "<section>" .data.<section>` (section-helper, dict-list-helpers, child-constructor, children-forwarding); a body, `text` fields and rendered sections arrive as safe HTML while other fields are typed values; the reserved `.deck`/`.slide`/`.item`/`.raw`/`.data` context (`.deck.title`, `.deck.author`, `.deck.date`, `.deck.properties`, `.slide.number` string, `.slide.total` integer, `.item.*`, `.raw.<field>`/`.raw.body`, `.data.<section>`) and the reserved names `deck`/`slide`/`item`/`raw`/`data` (template-language, template-context, item-context, raw-source-context, section-data-context, requirements.constraint.reserved-template-names);
- `{{ template }}` layout composition: a library layout's name is its root-qualified layout file path (`<layer root>/slides|sections/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl`), a layout may include layouts of its own layer, a local-layer layout may include an external-layer layout by that layer's root-qualified path, an external-layer layout naming a local-layer layout is a templates error, and an included layout executes in the invoking layout's namespace so its `media` binds to the invoking layer while a `section` call rebinds to the target's layer (guide-layout-composition, example-layout-composition, library-layer-references);
- themes: token-only stylesheets, no built-in theme, `default` implicit, available themes are the merged library's `themes/` directories across both layers (theme-selection, layered-template-library, global.constraint.theme-tokens-only, global.constraint.no-per-slide-styling); library reference rules — the layout `{{media}}` helper (relative to `media/`), theme-owned files referenced from CSS by relative `url()`, and the reserved `media:` (shared `media/` tree) and `theme:<name>/` (another theme's files) URL prefixes, both valid for `url()` and `@import` (template-media, theme-media-references);
- validation and errors: fail-fast, exactly one error naming file, line, path, what is wrong and how to fix it, the same text in terminal and browser (error-reporting, whole-deck-validation, closest-match-suggestions);
- the CLI: `init`, `init-library`, `config`, `start`, `templates`, `templates <name>`, `upgrade`, `version`, `help` (cli-commands), with `kalide start` as the validation gate.

The guide MUST instruct agents to discover a project's real templates with `kalide templates` / `kalide templates <name>` and to validate with `kalide start` rather than guessing template names, field names or themes. A build/test-time self-test guards each guide against drift from the implemented format (agent-guide-drift-check).

AC:
- immediately after `kalide init`, `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` and a referencing `AGENTS.md` exist at the project root and `kalide start` still validates and serves the starter deck cleanly (the files are inert to the loader);
- immediately after `kalide init ../lib` against a resolvable library, the guide pair exists at the deck root and `kalide start` validates and serves from `../lib`;
- beyond the deck's seed content, `kalide init` writes exactly the guide pair — `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` and the `AGENTS.md` pointer — and no other guide-related file;
- the example snippets in the guide, copied into a fresh project, validate via `kalide start` without error;
- the guide documents the reserved `media:` and `theme:<name>/` theme-CSS prefixes and an example using them (theme-media-references);
- the guide documents the `media`/`section`/`dict`/`list`/`child`/`children` helpers — including that `child`/`children` name a declared section of the `section` call's target and choose the template with a `template` key (child-constructor, children-forwarding) — the `.raw`/`.data` context and the reserved names `raw`/`data`, so it stays drift-free (agent-guide-drift-check);
- the guide documents `{{ template }}` layout composition per guide-layout-composition;
- the guide documents that the deck's library is one merged set drawn from up to two layers — the external `templates:` library and the deck-root `templates/`, both loaded when both are present, with no precedence (layered-template-library);
- the guide documents that a name carried in both layers — a slide or section in the shared template namespace, a theme, or a shared `media/` path — is a validation error naming the name and both paths, never an override (library-layer-uniqueness);
- the guide documents that a local template's `section` helper call resolves against the merged section namespace while every other reference stays within its own layer, and that the external library is self-contained (library-layer-references);
- scaffolding into a directory that already holds an author `AGENTS.md` succeeds and leaves it byte-for-byte unchanged when it already carries the reference string, or appends the reference line and preserves the remainder when it does not (agent-guide-reference); `AGENTS_KALIDE.md` is written only when absent.
